    no sending domain, no bounce handling, no unsubscribe compliance. Cheap and fully testable. But it only works for
    people who already open the app regularly; for anyone else the notification is invisible, which arguably defeats
    "get notified."
  - [1mEmail too[0m — reaches people who aren't in the app, which is the actual point of notifying. Costs a provider account,
    a sending domain with SPF/DKIM, bounce and complaint handling, unsubscribe links, and batching/digesting so an
    active task doesn't send someone fifteen emails in an hour. That batching logic is the part people underestimate.
  - [1mWeb push too[0m — browser-native popups, no provider bill. Costs a service worker, VAPID keys, a permission prompt
    users frequently decline, and subscriptions that silently expire and need pruning. Reaches only users who opted in
    and have the browser running.

  What's cheap to change later: adding email or push [3mafter[0m an in-app inbox exists is additive, because the inbox already
  forces you to model "notification event, for this user, unread." What's expensive: retrofitting per-user channel
  preferences and digest batching once notifications are being generated in volume — though that's still far cheaper
  than retrofitting sharing.

  My recommendation: [1min-app inbox for v1, email in the same design but explicitly phase 2.[0m It gets the event model and
  the subscription semantics right against something you can test end-to-end without deliverability infrastructure, and
  email becomes a delivery adapter over an event stream that already works.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Channels [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhich delivery channels should the design cover, and which ship in v1?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mIn-app now, email phase 2 (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mBuild the event model, subscriptions, and bell/inbox UI first. Design so email is an adapter added later without [39m
     [38;5;246mre-architecting. Fully testable with no external provider.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m In-app + email in v1
     [38;5;246mBoth ship together. Adds provider setup, sending domain, bounce handling, unsubscribe, and digest batching to the [39m
     [38;5;246minitial scope — roughly doubles the build.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m In-app only, ever
     [38;5;246mDeliberately scoped to users who open the app. Smallest system; no delivery infrastructure at all. We drop the [39m
     [38;5;246mchannel abstraction entirely as YAGNI.[39m
  [38;5;246m4.[39m In-app + web push
     [38;5;246mBrowser notifications instead of email. No provider bill, but needs a service worker, VAPID keys, permission UX, [39m
     [38;5;246mand subscription expiry handling.[39m
  [38;5;246m5. Type something.[39m
